# Prove that two copies of the SAME unidentified book occupy one pack entry,
# while a DIFFERENT unidentified book is its own entry. Oracle for inc-elhn.
#
#   tools/headless.sh tools/keys/book-stack.keys 1
#
# THE BOOKS MUST BE UNIDENTIFIED, which is why this script uses wizard mode's
# [e] "Acquire Unknown Item" and NOT "Item Acquisition". Item::Create gives
# every item a fresh GenNum (src/Item.cpp:89), and Item::operator== (src/Item.cpp)
# only lets items from different generation groups stack when they are fully
# identified (KN_MAGIC|KN_PLUS|KN_BLESS). "Acquire Unknown Item"
# (src/Debug.cpp:659-668) clears Known to 0 before the pickup, so two books
# taken this way carry different GenNums and Known 0 -- exactly the case the
# fix at the T_BOOK case restores to easy-stacking.
#
# WHY BOOKS. T_BOOK is the only easy-stackable type whose candidates exist at
# level 0 in a form a scripted walk can reach by name: the T_BOOK glyph category
# (ItemGlyphs, src/Tables.cpp:320) offers "Fundamentals of Magick", "Adventurers'
# Arts", "Mystic Aegis", "Tome of Magic" and more at minlev 0. Spellbooks are
# also not identified by flavor, so a run that fails to make them unknown still
# names them -- but the check would then read two entries only if the GenNum
# guard bit, so the guide name "? spellbook" on the dump is what tells the two
# apart. The check re-reads that "?" marker and calls a run without it
# INCONCLUSIVE.
#
# THE ACQUISITION LIST HAS NO MENU LETTERS (TextTerm::AcquisitionPrompt,
# src/Term.cpp), so neither @choose nor @cursorto can reach a row -- walking
# DOWN is the only way in, and that is the idiom tools/keys/acquire-blast-
# circlet.keys documents. @until stops on the pass where the wanted name first
# appears at the bottom of the three-line window; one further DOWN moves the
# selection onto it. Adding a book to lib/sp_books.irh ahead of one of these
# moves the rows, so @expect nails each name to the row before ENTER takes it.
#
# A book that will not stack lands in the In-the-Air slot and opens the
# inventory manager, which refuses to close while that slot is full; the
# @while pair stows it and leaves. A book that stacks (after the fix, once the
# first copy is already in the pack) is auto-stowed by Character::PickUp
# (src/Inv.cpp:580-596) and neither @while fires. The "? spellbook" in the
# stow pattern is deliberate: without it the air-slot text never clears and
# the loop runs out its passes.
#
# One pack dump, scrolled to the "Other Items" section where a book too large
# for a belt slot is listed. The test is the whole dump:
#
#   before the fix  11) ? spellbook [Mystic Aegis]
#                   12) ? spellbook [Mystic Aegis]      <- two entries
#                   13) ? spellbook [Tome of Magic]
#   after the fix   11) 2 ? spellbooks [Mystic Aegis]   <- one entry, count 2
#                   12) ? spellbook [Tome of Magic]     <- on its own

@include chargen.keys

# --- two copies of "Mystic Aegis" -------------------------------------------
w
@until "Wizard Mode Options" y
@choose "Acquire Unknown Item"
RIGHT*7
@until "Spellbook [Mystic Aegis]" DOWN
@expect "Spellbook [Mystic Aegis]"
DOWN
ENTER
@while "In the Air':? spellbook" s
@while "Ready Hand" ESC

w
@until "Wizard Mode Options" y
@choose "Acquire Unknown Item"
RIGHT*7
@until "Spellbook [Mystic Aegis]" DOWN
@expect "Spellbook [Mystic Aegis]"
DOWN
ENTER
@while "In the Air':? spellbook" s
@while "Ready Hand" ESC

# --- one copy of a different book, "Tome of Magic" --------------------------
w
@until "Wizard Mode Options" y
@choose "Acquire Unknown Item"
RIGHT*7
@until "Spellbook [Tome of Magic]" DOWN
@expect "Spellbook [Tome of Magic]"
DOWN
ENTER
@while "In the Air':? spellbook" s
@while "Ready Hand" ESC

i
PGDN*1
@dump:pack
ESC

@quit
